{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cggt8Ep5vu5JNJK83JzGB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral ZnO:Ni²⁺ I get **Dq ≈ 515 cm⁻¹ (10Dq ≈ 5 150 cm⁻¹)** and **B ≈ 790 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Approach**\n\nTetrahedral d⁸ has the same term pattern as octahedral d². The ground state is ³T₁(F), so I used the d² Tanabe–Sugano expressions with Dq set to the tetrahedral Dq. All triplet energies are measured from ³F.\n\n- ³T₂ lies at +2Dq.\n- The two ³T₁ terms mix through the matrix\n  | −6Dq   4Dq |\n  |  4Dq   15B |\n  Its eigenvalues are E± = (15B − 6Dq)/2 ± ½√[(15B + 6Dq)² + 64Dq²].\n\n**Transitions from the ground state ³T₁(1)**\n\n- ν(³T₂) = 5Dq − 7.5B + ½√(225B² + 180BDq + 100Dq²) = 4 400 cm⁻¹\n- ν(³T₁(2)) = √(225B² + 180BDq + 100Dq²) = 15 500 cm⁻¹\n\n**Solving**\n\n1. Putting the second equation into the first gives 5Dq − 7.5B + 7 750 = 4 400. So Dq = 1.5B − 670.\n2. Substituting this into the second equation gives 720B² − 321 600B − 195 360 000 = 0.\n3. The positive root is B ≈ 790 cm⁻¹, which gives Dq ≈ 515 cm⁻¹.\n\nBoth observed bands are spin-allowed triplet → triplet transitions, so their energies depend only on Dq and B. The given ratio C/B = 4.5 therefore does not affect the fit. It only sets C = 4.5 × 790 ≈ 3 556 cm⁻¹.\n\n{\"Dq\": 515, \"B\": 790, \"C\": 3556, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fitted both spin-allowed triplet bands with the d2-analogue Tanabe-Sugano equations for tetrahedral d8, and C comes from C/B = 4.5.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1698,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1698,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c072e5e7-21ad-4a